+        "content": "# Session 5 record (B5: closing the loop \u2014 phase (f) maintain, evals, scans, gate (f), observability; steps 33\u201343; decision 26) \u2014 kept as written at the end of session 5\n\n## Summary\n- Session 5 (2026-09-25, one cloud session) built build stage **B5** in one draft PR against `main`, plugin **0.2.19**: phase (f) maintain end to end \u2014 the deterministic detection script and its bands (step 37, decisions 14, 15, 26), the read-only diagnosis (`/sdlc-maintain`), the gated routes with the two shipped runbooks, gate (f) as the owner's triage with the dismissal store (step 39, decision 25), the incident record (`lessons/`, step 36), the eval runner with the first four framework cases (step 35), the weekly security review with the deterministic scanners (step 38, decision 16), every hook logging every decision with the log as a CI artifact (41.1), the counters in the daily digest (42.2), the mock convention (34) and the worktree record (33). The tag and the release page follow the merge (`sdlc-tag.yml`); the owner bumps the root and the sample pins to 0.2.19 (guardrail files).\n- The loop is the article's (p.42\u201345) with the framework's gates: a daily workflow computes the CI test failure rate per day from the runs API, judges the last eight points against the rolling baseline with the four Western Electric rules (no model), logs every run, and at 2\u03c3 or 3\u03c3 files one incident change per metric and runs the diagnosis, which writes the incident intent and proposes a route; a pre-approved runbook (quarantine a flaky test, open a revert PR) runs at once on a branch of its own and opens its PR, a `go` route parks the intent PR with \"Go requested\" until the owner's `sdlc:go`, a rehearsed rollback on a declared production is pre-approved (decision 26) \u2014 the dispatch is a CI step of its own after the model's, judging by the default branch's bands, with the runbook secrets in that step's environment only. The intent PR carries `sdlc:f-ready` and `incident`; merge = fix now (gate (a)), `schedule` = later, close with a comment = dismiss (a dismissal PR against the default branch). Nothing notifies.\n- Tests: 1096 tests (from 725), ruff clean, `claude plugin validate .` clean; unit tests on synthetic series for the statistics, on a fake runs API for the source, on temporary repositories with a bare remote for the runbooks, the dispatcher, the dismissal and the gate; a fake `claude` for the eval runner and the scan; the workflows parsed and their contracts asserted. Nothing of phase (f) has run live yet: the live checks are listed below, and the first is one `workflow_dispatch` of `sdlc-detect.yml` with `force_tier: 2` on the sample repository once it pins 0.2.19.\n- Delegation as `docs/MODEL_ALLOCATION.md` \u00a75 planned: Opus wrote the statistics, the source, the runbooks, the eval runner, the scan package, the 35.2 research and four test files from Fable's specs; Fable wrote the design (routes and authorization, the dispatcher, the dismissal store, the finding record, gate (f), the CLI), the prompts, the workflows, the observability change, the counters and the docs; a fresh-context Opus review of the whole diff ran before the commit and its findings were fixed (its findings and their fixes are listed under \"Fresh-context review\" below).\n- Left: the live checks of the loop (below), the owner-optional guardrail lines (below), the deploy/status/rollback tools (choice 35, when an adapter names a real deployment), the `Diff.files` conversion and the per-decision panel cost (untouched code), an owner `accept` verb for a review finding (a new decision, if wanted), the release-page backfill (optional), step 33 (not built, decision 17) and step 40 (optional). No build stage remains; `HANDOFF.md` is now the session-6 brief (the phase (f) shakedown: the live checks, the fixes they force, the eval cases from real runs), written at the close of session 5 from the \"Left\" list below; the session-5 brief is kept as `docs/handoffs/session-5-B5.md`.\n\n## Definition of done (handoff)\n| Item | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `python tasks.py check` green (report the count) and `claude plugin validate .` clean | 1096 tests, ruff check and format clean, validate clean (Linux, this session; the Windows run is the owner's). |\n| Detection statistics unit-tested on synthetic series; the three tiers act as decisions 14 and 26 say; the diagnose run is read-only and writes an incident intent in the template's shape; gate (f) has its checks | Done: `plugin/detect/stats.py` (45 tests: window boundaries, insufficient baseline, sigma 0, each rule, direction, min_sigma, ties, a run that ended early), `routes.py` + `runbooks.py` + `cli.py` (the tiers: 2\u03c3 only `pull_request`; 3\u03c3 a listed route; `preapproved` runs now, `go` records \"go-requested\", decision 26 flips a rehearsed rollback on `deploy.production: true`), `/sdlc-maintain` (no `Edit`; `design_scope` refuses a committed source file at (f)), `CHECKS_BY_PHASE[\"f\"]` = artifacts (with `## Evidence`), design_scope, guardrails, risk_list, detection, route. |\n| The eval runner runs a suite non-interactively and fails on a regression; the framework suite has its first cases | Done: `plugin/evals/run.py` (a workspace copy per case, never `--bare`, the checks in Python, a JSON report, exit 1 below `--min-pass-rate`), `evals/check.py` in the template, `sdlc-evals.yml` (template) and `framework-evals.yml` (this repository, over the initialised fixture), four cases in `evals/cases/`. Never run against a real `claude` yet (a live check). |\n| The weekly security review and the dismissal store exist and are tested; the deterministic scanners are in the template's CI | Done: `plugin/scan/` (`prompt`, `route`, `scanners`, `cli review | route | scanners`), `sdlc-scan.yml`, `changes/.dismissed.json` (`plugin/detect/dismissals.py`, shared by detection and scan findings; the abandon workflow writes it through a dismissal PR), `pip-audit` + `bandit` / `npm audit` reported as artifacts. |\n| Lessons, the mock convention and the worktree record exist; every hook logs its decisions; the two counters have their report | Done: `template/lessons/README.md` + `/sdlc-deploy` step 0b + `/sdlc-maintain` step 1; `changes/<id>-<slug>/mock/` in `changes/README.md` and the (c)/(d) prompts; step 33 recorded below; `_common.run_hook` logs every decision (the production gate keeps its own line) and every phase workflow uploads the log; `pr/counters.py` as the last section of the digest. |\n| OPERATING_MODEL says what the code does for phase (f); \u00a710 row 26 moves into the body | Done: \u00a74.1 (the (f) row rewritten), \u00a74.2 (rows for `sdlc-detect.yml`, `sdlc-runbook.yml`, `sdlc-scan.yml`, `sdlc-evals.yml`; the abandon and digest rows extended), \u00a78 (the labels, the evidence files, the phase-f rule), \u00a79 (`bands.yaml`, `maintain.runbooks` with `command` and the two shipped runbooks, `lessons/`), \u00a710 (row 26 \"Built\"). |\n| `docs/PROGRESS.md` for session 5 with the \u22645-bullet summary on top and the list of what remains | This file. |\n\n## Steps\n| Step | Status | What exists / what is left |\n|---|---|---|\n| 33 Worktrees | not built, recorded | Decision 17: one person reviews at phase ends only, so a second stream waits on the same review; the framework's runs are unattended and serialised per change by the workflows' concurrency groups; a parallel worktree needs the conflict escalation of the guide's note designed first. Nothing in the code refers to worktrees. Revisit when review keeps up. |\n| 34 Design mock | done (convention) | `changes/<id>-<slug>/mock/` (changes/README.md); `/sdlc-build` step 4 reads it before the first screen and names the screenshot in `plan.md` Proof; `/sdlc-test` step 3 compares against it and reports a mock the folder lacks as a mismatch. |\n| 35 Evals | done, unproven live | 35.1 the case shape `evals/cases/<id>-<slug>/{prompt.md, checks.yaml, expected/}` with `command`, `file` (`exists`, `contains`, `not_contains`, `unchanged`, `equals`), `output` (`contains`, `not_contains`, `regex`) and `denied` checks, `setup`, `tools`, `permission_mode`, `max_turns`, `budget_usd`, `timeout_seconds`; 35.2 NOTES \u00a713a\u2013b (`claude plugin eval` read and not used); 35.3 `plugin/evals/run.py`, `template/evals/check.py`, `sdlc-evals.yml` (PRs to `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/**`, `evals/**`; nightly), `.github/workflows/framework-evals.yml` (PRs to `plugin/**`, `template/**`, `evals/**`; nightly; `--fixture`); 35.4 four cases: a protected path denied, the intent skill's shape, a secret kept out, verify before done. |\n| 36 Lessons | done | `template/lessons/README.md` (naming `lessons/<yyyy-mm>-<slug>.md`, the shape), `/sdlc-init` creates it; read first by `/sdlc-maintain`; written by `/sdlc-deploy` step 0b with the eval case (`plugin/evals/case.py new`) when an incident's fix ships, both listed in `plan.md`. |\n| 37 Detection and bands | done, unproven live | `plugin/detect/` (`stats`, `bands`, `source`, `finding`, `dismissals`, `routes`, `runbooks`, `cli`), `template/bands.yaml` (the optional `direction`, `min_sigma`), `template/sdlc.yaml: maintain.runbooks[].command`, `sdlc-detect.yml`, `sdlc-runbook.yml`, `/sdlc-maintain`, `run_phase.py --phase f`, gate (f). Decision 26 in `routes.authorization`. |\n| 38 Scans | done, unproven live | `plugin/scan/`, `sdlc-scan.yml` (weekly), the routing (choice 74), the scanners per language. |\n| 39 Gate (f) | done | Labels `incident`, `schedule`, `sdlc:go`; the human gate at (f) in every profile (`conventions.HUMAN_GATES`); the merge of the incident PR read as gate (a) (`run_phase.PHASE_BEFORE`); the dismissal on close (`sdlc-abandon.yml` \u2192 `detect/cli.py dismiss --open-pr`); a bands change only through a `bands.yaml` PR (the detection run never writes it; the dismissal store is a separate file). |\n| 40 Chat entry | optional, not built | Any bot that writes an intent and opens the PR joins the same queue; nothing built. |\n| 41 / 41.1 Observability | done | `_common.run_hook` logs every decision (hook, verdict, reason, tool, paths; `production_gate` passes `log=False` and keeps its own line); the phase workflows (design, build, test, deploy, fix, detect) upload `changes/**/evidence/hook-log.jsonl` and `changes/.hook-log.jsonl` as an artifact; the detection log and the eval and scan reports are artifacts too. |\n| 42 Indicators | done | 42.1 the PR body's counters line (unchanged); 42.2 `plugin/pr/counters.py` (first-pass merge share, fix iterations per shipped change, panel calls, abandoned, incidents filed / shipped / dismissed, dismissals in force) rendered by `pr/digest.py --root .` as the last section of the review-queue issue. |\n| 43 Reference | done | The guide's note extended: B5 closed the graph's tier 5. |\n\n## Decisions: none reopened, three readings recorded\nBuilt as the decisions say; where their text left room, the reading is recorded here for the owner to overrule: (i) decision 14's \"open a PR into the review gate\" at 3\u03c3 is read as the incident intent PR itself (the article's `pull_request` route), never a fix PR that skips gates (a) and (b) \u2014 decisions 18 and 21 keep (b) human, and the same reading applies to a bounded security finding of step 38 (choice 74); (ii) decision 25's \"close with a reason\": the reason is the last comment by a person on the PR before the close, and without one there is no dismissal (the finding may return next run) \u2014 a dismissal without a reason would mute a metric silently, which p.47 step 4 rules out; (iii) decision 26's \"rehearsed rollback\": any runbook whose name contains `rollback` with a dated `rehearsed_at` on a project with `deploy.production: true`; the article's `runbook:rollback-deploy` is the template's example.\n\n## Choices made where the pack left it open (continuing session 4's numbering)\n66. **Runtime of the loop: a CI step** (37.4): the scheduled `sdlc-detect.yml`, not an Agent SDK service \u2014 the trigger table of \u00a74.2 is workflows, no webhook source exists, and decision 7 forbids a runtime dependency. `workflow_dispatch(force_tier)` rehearses the whole loop on demand and is recorded as `forced` in the detection record.\n67. **Observations and the window** (37.1, 37.2): one observation per UTC day = failed runs / counted runs (completed, `success` or `failure`, events push / pull_request / schedule / workflow_dispatch, workflows not named `SDLC \u2026`; `source: github-actions:<workflow name>` narrows to one); `baseline: rolling_<N>d` is the window; the baseline never contains the points it judges: the last eight points of the window are the tail the rules read, the rest the baseline (mean, sample standard deviation); fewer than eight baseline points \u2192 tier 0 \"insufficient baseline\".\n68. **The rules and their tiers** (37.1): the four classic Western Electric rules, each satisfied by a run ending at the latest point (an older breach inside the tail was reported by an earlier run): `we1` one point beyond 3\u03c3 \u2192 3\u03c3; `we2` two of three beyond 2\u03c3 \u2192 2\u03c3; `we4` eight points on one side of the mean \u2192 2\u03c3 (a shift of the mean is the slow drift p.43 wants diagnosed); `we3` four of five beyond 1\u03c3 \u2192 1\u03c3 (the early warning that only logs). Ties: the highest tier, then `we1`, `we2`, `we4`, `we3`. `direction: above` by default (a failure rate is worse when higher); `sigma == 0` makes any point above the mean an infinite breach \u2014 the article's own example (p.45: one bad day after a clean month) \u2014 and `min_sigma` is the owner's floor.\n69. **One finding per metric at a time** (37.4, p.44 step 6): the detection skips filing while an incident of the metric is in flight (a remote `sdlc/<id>/a` with a detection record for the metric, not abandoned, and not at phase e on the default branch) or while its signature is dismissed; a finding is \"the same\" when the same metric trips the same rule (`detect|metric|rule`), a detection dismissal expires after one baseline window, a scan dismissal never.\n70. **The proposal is the diagnosis's, the verdict the dispatcher's** (37.4, 37.5): the model writes `evidence/proposal.json` (route, args, rationale); `detect/cli.py dispatch` validates it against the tier and `bands.yaml`, resolves the authorization (the bands' value; a stricter `go` in `sdlc.yaml: maintain.runbooks` wins; decision 26 on top) and acts; a refused proposal is rewritten once by the run and then parks with the reason. At 2\u03c3 the only route is `pull_request`; the intent PR is the diagnosis's whole artifact (p.44 step 5).\n71. **\"Go\" is a label** (37.4, decision 14): `sdlc:go` on the incident PR, read by `sdlc-runbook.yml`, performed once by `detect/cli.py go` when a person applied it (never the token or a `[bot]`), recorded as `status.yaml: runbook_authorized_by`, the label removed \u2014 the un-park labels' shape (decision 24). A comment is never a Go.\n72. **The two shipped runbooks never touch a test and never merge** (37.4, p.45): `quarantine-flaky-test` adds `--deselect <node id>` to pytest's `addopts` (`pytest.ini`, `pyproject.toml`, `tox.ini`, `setup.cfg`, in pytest's order; created when none exists) on `sdlc/<id>/quarantine` and opens the PR (Python projects only; a Node project gets \"unsupported\" and the intent PR alone); `revert-pr` reverts one commit of the default branch (`-m 1` for a merge) on `sdlc/<id>/revert-<sha7>` and opens the PR; both idempotent; a project's own runbook is a `command` under `maintain.runbooks`, run without a shell like `deploy.command`.\n73. **An incident change stays at phase f until its intent PR is merged** (39.1): the maintain run leaves `status.yaml: phase: f` on `sdlc/<id>/a`; the design workflow's guard accepts `f` as the phase before (b) (`PHASE_BEFORE`), so the owner's merge is gate (a) of that change (decision 25) with no write to the default branch; `read_status(on_default_branch=True)` needs nothing new. The PR title is `maintain(<id>): \u2026`, the gate label `sdlc:f-ready`, plus `incident`.\n74. **Every security finding is an incident intent** (38.1, decision 16): a bounded finding becomes a `change_type: fix` intent whose Proposed outcome is the patch, with the reviewer's suggested diff in `evidence/suggested-patch.diff` for the build run; a wide one a `feature` intent describing the class; at most three per weekly run; no route skips gates (a) and (b). The vulnerability-class eval (p.47 step 7) is the incident's eval of step 36. The scanners (`pip-audit`, `bandit`; `npm audit`) run in the same workflow, reported as artifacts, never a gate: the owner reads them; a finding worth a change is filed by the review.\n75. **The eval runner is the framework's own** (35.1, 35.2): `claude plugin eval` loads only the plugin in a throwaway workspace, has no custom-code graders, puts a repository into a run only through a Bash scaffold script, doubles the cost with its baseline arm and can be switched off server-side (NOTES \u00a713a); the framework's per-project suite tests the project's `CLAUDE.md`, hooks and settings (so the runner never passes `--bare`) with checks that are commands and file states. Cases are `prompt.md` + `checks.yaml`; the report is JSON; the pass rate is the job's verdict.\n76. **Every hook logs every decision** (41.1): `run_hook` appends the line (a hook that crashes logs its fail-closed block too); the cost is one `git rev-parse` per hook call to find the change's evidence folder, inside every hook's time budget; the production gate keeps computing its own path (choice 39). The log is evidence, committed with the change, and an artifact of every phase run.\n77. **The counters ride in the digest** (42.2): `pr/counters.py` over the change folders of the default-branch checkout; no report PR (the guide's \"you merge or ignore\" would be one more queue item), no model; the digest issue's last section.\n78. **`evals/check.py` locates the framework** (35.3): `--framework`, `$SDLC_FRAMEWORK_DIR`, or `framework/` beside `evals/` (the CI checkout); a project runs its suite by hand with the framework checkout it pins.\n79. **A bands change is a `bands.yaml` PR** (39.1, p.44 step 6 \"Dismissals tune the bands\"): the dismissal store mutes a metric + rule pair for one window; widening a band is the owner's edit of the file, reviewed like any project content; nothing in the loop writes `bands.yaml`.\n80. **The dispatcher is a CI step of its own and judges by the approved copies** (review finding 2): `sdlc-detect.yml` runs the model with `--diagnosis-only` (the intent and the proposal, committed) and then `detect/cli.py finish` (dispatch, gate (f), evidence, the PR) with the project's runbook secrets in that step's environment only; `dispatch`, `finish` and `go` read `bands.yaml` and `sdlc.yaml` from `origin/<default>` through `git show`, so an uncommitted edit in the session \u2014 a prompt injection in a failed-run log could ask for one \u2014 changes no authorization (decision 14 \"enforced, not remembered\"; decision 11).\n81. **A forced finding never pre-approves a runbook** (review finding 3): `force_tier` is a rehearsal with no breach behind it; decision 26's pre-approval and a bands `preapproved` are for a real 3\u03c3, so every runbook route of a forced finding resolves to `go` (the intent PR still opens; the owner's `sdlc:go` runs the runbook if wanted).\n82. **Only complete UTC days are judged** (review finding 6): the daily run at 05:41 UTC drops today's observation before evaluating, so the latest point is yesterday's full day; the log still records every run.\n83. **A pending dismissal already mutes** (review finding 7): the detection reads the dismissal entries of every `origin/sdlc/<id>/dismiss` branch beside the default branch's store, and the incident folders on the default branch beside the remote `sdlc/<id>/a` branches, so neither the day between a close and the dismissal PR's merge nor a deleted merged head refiles a finding.\n\n## Live checks the owner can do now (not automatable here)\n16. **The loop rehearsed** (37): on the sample repository at 0.2.19, `workflow_dispatch` of `SDLC detect (f)` with `force_tier: 2` \u2192 a green job, the detection log artifact, a change `000N-ci-test-failure-rate-breached-2sigma-\u2026` on `sdlc/000N/a`, the intent PR `maintain(000N): \u2026` with `sdlc:f-ready` + `incident`, \"Finding \u2026 forced by a rehearsal\" in its body. Then `force_tier: 3`: the diagnosis may propose `runbook:revert-pr` or `quarantine-flaky-test` (the sample is Python) and the runbook's PR appears, or `pull_request`.\n17. **The three triage verbs** (39): merge the incident PR \u2192 the design job starts (the guard reads `f` as `a`); on another one apply `schedule` \u2192 nothing runs, the digest lists it; close a third with a comment \u2192 the abandon job records `abandoned` and opens `dismiss(000N): \u2026` against `main`; merge it and the next detection run says \"dismissed until \u2026\".\n18. **The Go** (37.4, decision 14): on a rehearsal that proposed `runbook:rollback-deploy` (declare a runbook with a harmless `command` under `maintain.runbooks`, `authorization: go`), the PR parks with \"Go requested\"; apply `sdlc:go` \u2192 the runbook job runs it once, removes the label, the PR returns to `sdlc:f-ready`; a second `sdlc:go` by a `[bot]` would be left in place (not testable by hand).\n19. **Decision 26** (37.3): the same with `deploy.production: true` and `rehearsed_at: 2026-09-25` on the runbook \u2192 it runs at dispatch, no Go asked, the record names decision 26.\n20. **The scan** (38): `workflow_dispatch` of `SDLC scan` \u2192 the review's findings file and the scanner reports as artifacts; at most three `maintain(\u2026)` intent PRs with `security:` titles; close one with a comment \u2192 its signature in the dismissal PR; the next run skips it.\n21. **The evals** (35): `workflow_dispatch` of `SDLC evals` on the sample (an empty suite: green, \"no cases\"). `Framework evals` on this repository ran live on the PR (2026-09-25, the OAuth token, $0.44): 3 of 4 passed \u2014 the intent skill's shape (6 turns), the secret kept out (1 turn), verify before done (13 turns, the tests run and their output in the answer); case 0001 failed for the wrong reason: the agent read the fixture's `CLAUDE.md` rule and declined in one turn, so no Edit reached the hook and the `denied: Edit` check found no denial while the file stayed unchanged. The case now asserts the behaviour (the file unchanged) like case 0003; the hook's denial stays proven by `tests/test_hooks.py`. The second run (head eda699d, $0.58): 2 of 4 \u2014 case 0001 failed again on the `not_contains \"line added\"` check a refusal also trips (dropped: the file's bytes are the one check), and case 0004 passed its tests in 23 turns but paraphrased the result instead of pasting the summary line (the prompt now names the line to end with; the runner's failed output check keeps the answer's tail so a red run is diagnosable from the log). Both are eval-authoring lessons, not framework bugs: the first cases were written before any run, and the article's suite is built from real runs (p.30 step 1).\n22. **The hook log** (41.1): any phase run's artifact `sdlc-hook-log-<run id>` holds one line per hook decision; the change's `evidence/hook-log.jsonl` shows the same lines committed.\n23. **The digest's counters** (42.2): the next daily digest ends with \"## Counters\".\nStill owed from earlier sessions: the sample's first close-without-merge at gates (a)\u2013(e) (live check 8), the watch setting and the test-file lock on Windows (session 3).\n\n## Known gaps (carried)\n- Nothing of phase (f), the evals or the scan has run against a real `claude` or GitHub: every model-driven half is prose exercised by a fake; the live checks above are the proof. The runs API's `created` filter and the `Link` paging are used as the REST description documents them (NOTES \u00a711a's sources), unverified live.\n- A forged `evidence/runbook-<name>.json` with `status: ran` still passes the `route` check for a pre-approved route without a runbook having run (a `go` route now needs `authorized_by`, a shipped runbook its PR or a reason): the record is what the run committed, like every other evidence file; the runbook's own PR is the artifact the owner triages. In CI the record is written by the `finish` step, never by the model's session.\n- `open_incidents` reads the remote branches the checkout knows (`fetch-depth: 0` in CI; `git fetch origin` by hand) and the default branch's incident folders; a by-hand detection without a fetch may refile an incident that is open on the remote only.\n- The `Diff.files` conversion (`approved_artifact`, `gate.py`'s `sdlc.yaml` check, `policy.py`), the per-decision panel cost (choice 52) and the CI review-before-artifact order for a non-`none` adapter stay as session 4 left them: none of that code was touched.\n- The quarantine runbook covers pytest; a Node project's flaky test has no runbook (the intent PR alone), by design until a project needs one.\n- The scanners install `pip-audit` and `bandit` (or run `npm audit`) on the runner every week: CI dependencies of the project's workflow, not of the plugin (decision 7 is about the plugin's runtime); a project may pin them in its own workflow copy.\n\n## Guardrail lines for the owner (this session cannot edit these files)\n- Root `sdlc.yaml`: pin `0.2.19` after the merge (and the sample repository's `main`); the sample needs the new workflows (`/sdlc-init` reports `sdlc-detect.yml`, `sdlc-runbook.yml`, `sdlc-scan.yml`, `sdlc-evals.yml` as `created`, the six phase workflows as `outdated` \u2014 replace them with the template copies to get the hook-log artifact). **Done 2026-09-25** (see \"Between sessions 5 and 6\" below).\n- Root `CLAUDE.md`, \"Architecture\": `plugin/detect/` (the detection statistics, the routes and runbooks, the dismissal store, the (f) CLI), `plugin/evals/` (the eval runner and the incident case skeleton), `plugin/scan/` (the weekly security review and the scanners) \u2014 three lines to add; and `template/`: `lessons/`, the detect, runbook, scan and evals workflows. **Done 2026-09-25**; the three `plugin/` lines (24\u201326) lack the backticks of their neighbours \u2014 cosmetic, the owner's choice.\n- Still optional from session 4: `gate.max_panel_calls: 4` in the root `sdlc.yaml`; the template's withdrawal note in place of its `# lite:` comment; the sample's `CLAUDE.md` Layout line for `mean`.\n\n## Fresh-context review (Opus, between the two commits of the PR)\nThe review read the whole diff against the handoff, the decisions and the operating model, and reported sixteen findings. Every one was fixed in the second commit, with a test where the review said none existed; the two it judged low and left as is are recorded in the known gaps:\n1. **The scheduled run never ran** (critical): the workflow passes `--force-tier \"\"` on a schedule, which the parser refused. Fixed (`\"\"` = force nothing) and tested; the template test that had locked the run line in now also asserts the empty value.\n2. **The model could change a route's authorization, and deploy secrets sat in its environment** (high): the dispatcher ran inside the model's session and read the checkout's `bands.yaml`. Fixed: in CI the dispatch, gate (f) and the PR are a step of their own after the model's (`detect/cli.py finish`; the runbook secrets live in that step's `env:` only; the model's session runs `/sdlc-maintain <id> --diagnosis-only`), and the dispatcher judges by the default branch's `bands.yaml` and `sdlc.yaml` through `git show`, never the checkout's (choice 80). Gate (f)'s `route` check reads the merge base's `bands.yaml` when the branch changed it.\n3. **A rehearsal could trigger a real rollback** (high): `force_tier: 3` plus decision 26. Fixed: a forced finding never pre-approves a runbook (`routes.apply_forced`; choice 81).\n4. **Merging a parked incident PR started nothing** (high): the design guard refused a change at `f` with a park. Fixed: the merge lifts the park (`run_phase.guard`) and `read_status(on_default_branch=True)` reads a merged `f` as gate (a) passed (`merged_at_gate_a`).\n5. **The built-in runbooks failed on a declared production** (medium-high): the hook log the production gate appends to made the tree \"dirty\". Fixed: files under `changes/` never block a runbook.\n6. **A partial day was judged** (medium): the 05:41 UTC run compared today's first hours with full days. Fixed: only complete UTC days are judged (choice 82).\n7. **The same finding came back** (medium): a merged incident whose head GitHub deleted, and a closed one whose dismissal PR was not merged yet. Fixed: `open_incidents` also reads the default branch's incident folders, and a pending `sdlc/<id>/dismiss` branch already suppresses the signature (choice 83).\n8. **The digest missed the runbook and dismissal PRs** (medium): they carry `incident` and no gate label. Fixed: a \"Phase (f): runbook and dismissal PRs\" section; the dismissal PR is labelled `incident`.\n9. **Two counters were zero by construction** (medium): abandoned and dismissed never reach the default branch. Fixed: the rows the default branch cannot tell were dropped and the section says so.\n10. **This repository's copy of the eval workflow was always red** (medium): no `evals/check.py` here. Fixed: the copy removed; `framework-evals.yml` is this repository's; the template test says so.\n11. **The Go and the route check were too trusting** (medium-low). Fixed: `go` runs only a route whose record says `go-requested`; a `ran` record on a `go` route needs `authorized_by`, a shipped runbook's needs its PR or a reason.\n12. **A stray hook log was committed** (low): a test run before the test fixture existed left `changes/.hook-log.jsonl`. Fixed: removed, ignored here (`.gitignore`), the fixture `tests/conftest.py` keeps every test's log in its temp dir.\n13. **Eval workspaces carried the framework checkout** (low). Fixed: `framework/` ignored.\n14. **Case 0003 tested the behaviour, not the hook** (low). Fixed by saying so in the case: the credential must not land whichever half refuses.\n15. **`dismiss` on a detached HEAD or a second close** (low). Fixed: the restore point is the commit when detached; a pending `origin/sdlc/<id>/dismiss` is continued.\n16. **Docs** (low): NOTES \u00a713c said `upload-artifact` had no v5 without a source (rewritten as an unverified choice to check on the next bump); OPERATING_MODEL \u00a74.1 lacked `Bash(gh run list *)` (added). The review confirmed the statistics against a hand-computed series, the decisions in the routing code, the token rule in every workflow, the credentials only on the model steps, and that a maintain run cannot commit source and pass.\n\nA red commit slipped out after the review: the second commit (e7fd9dd) was pushed on the tail of the test log instead of the exit code \u2014 the mistake the handoff names \u2014 while its run had one failure (`test_security_baseline_check_finds_secrets_and_dangerous_calls`: a comment line of the new detect workflow, \"holds no deploy secret: a project\", matched the secrets hook's credential-assignment pattern on the initialised fixture), and its message claims 1103 passed. The third commit rewords the comment, and its checks below are from a run whose exit code was read.\n\n## Left (no build stage remains)\n- The live checks 16\u201323 and the owed 8; the deploy/status/rollback tools when an adapter names a real deployment (choice 35); the `Diff.files` conversion; the per-decision panel cost; an owner `accept` verb for a review finding (a new decision, if wanted); the release-page backfill for v0.2.15\u2013v0.2.17 (optional); a runbook for a Node flaky test when a Node project needs one; step 33 (decision 17) and step 40 (optional). The session-6 `HANDOFF.md` was written from this list