-## 25. Facts read for plugin 0.2.30 (2026-10-02): the review panel's enforcement
-
-- **A sub-agent's tool calls fire the same PreToolUse hooks, and the input names the sub-agent** (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks, read 2026-10-02): "When a subagent calls a tool, tool events such as `PreToolUse` and `PostToolUse` fire the same configured hooks as in the main conversation, and the input carries the `agent_id` and `agent_type` common input fields that identify the subagent." `agent_id` is "Present only when the hook fires inside a subagent call". The `panel_blind` hook does not depend on it: its rule (no Read or Grep of one blind verdict while the other is missing) holds for every caller. SubagentStop's input fields and a model field in a sub-agent's transcript are not documented on the hooks or sub-agents pages.
-- **`modelUsage` names the sub-agents' models** (verified, not documented): the sample's change 0002 ran its panel at (b), (c) and (e), and `claude-b.json`, `claude-c.json` and `claude-e.json` list `claude-opus-5` beside the session's `claude-sonnet-5`; `claude-d.json`, `claude-fix.json` and `claude-review.json`, runs without a panel decision, list `claude-sonnet-5` only, as do all of change 0001's runs. The session's model is the one with the largest output (about 128,000 output tokens against 11,000 for opus at (b)). The 0.2.30 model check reads it per run, not per advocate call.
-- **`subagent_stats` counts sub-agents by type, without models** (verified, `claude-b.json` of change 0002): `{"by_type": {"general-purpose": 4, "sdlc:adversarial-reviewer": 6}, "completed": 10, ...}`. A plugin agent's type is its namespaced name, `sdlc:adversarial-reviewer`.
-- **The sample's four panel decisions all have both member files with a `## Verdict`** (verified on the sample's `main`: b-1, b-2, c-1 and e-1 of change 0002), so the stricter `record` and gate rules of 0.2.30 would have passed every decision taken live so far. Their ledger lines carry no `item_n`, so the gate does not re-judge them.
-- **A sub-agent's own transcript names the model of each reply** (observed on Claude Code 2.1.287, this container; not documented): the session's transcript is `<projects dir>/<session id>.jsonl`, and each sub-agent's is `<projects dir>/<session id>/subagents/agent-<agent id>.jsonl`, the same id the hook input carries as `agent_id`; every `"type": "assistant"` line carries `message.model` (the research sub-agent of this session: `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` on each reply). The `panel_blind` hook reads it when the devil's advocate writes its verdict and keeps the result in `<phase>-<n>-advocate.model.json`. **To confirm on the next CI panel run** (the workflows pin 2.1.278): that the record there names the advocate's model. If the layout differs, the record says "the sub-agent's transcript was not found", `record` refuses the item and it parks for the owner, so a different layout fails safe.
-- **The blindness hook's cost** (measured here, Linux): about 45 ms per ordinary Read or Grep, against 16 ms for a bare `python -c pass` and 75–97 ms for each of the three edit hooks; about 46 ms on an ordinary Bash call or edit, where it now also runs, so an edit runs four hooks; it reads the payload (and, for a Grep or a shell command that mentions the panel, scans `changes/*/evidence/panel/`) with the standard library before loading the shared hook code.
